## Coding Standards

Generate:
- Clean code
- Modular code
- Reusable code
- Typed code

Avoid:
- Dead code
- Overengineering
- Complex abstractions
- Premature optimization

---

## Architecture Rules

Always follow:

Feature First Structure

Example:

web/
├─ app/
├─ features/
├─ components/
├─ services/
├─ lib/
└─ types/

Keep components small.

Keep business logic separated.

Never place API logic directly inside UI components.

---

## API Rules

Use REST APIs.

Return:

{
  "success": true,
  "data": {}
}

Errors:

{
  "success": false,
  "message": ""
}

Always validate inputs.

---

## UI Rules

UI should be:

- Clean
- Modern
- Fast
- Municipality friendly

Avoid:
- Fancy animations
- Heavy effects
- Unnecessary libraries

---

## Git Rules

Commit frequently.

Good examples:

feat: add dashboard layout
feat: add map integration
fix: resolve api error
docs: update readme
chore: setup cursor rules

Bad examples:

update
last
test
fix

---

## Documentation Rules

Every major feature must include:

- Purpose
- Usage
- Architecture note

README must always be updated.

---

## AI Usage Rules

Document:

- Cursor usage
- Cursor prompts
- Cursor rules
- Cursor SDK usage
- AI workflow

Assume README will be reviewed by judges.

---

## Privacy Rules

Never implement:

- Face recognition
- Person tracking
- Vehicle tracking
- Plate recognition

Always prioritize KVKK compliance.

---

## Hackathon Strategy

If multiple solutions exist:

Choose the one that:

- Can be implemented faster
- Is easier to demo
- Provides public benefit
- Produces visible output

Working MVP > Perfect Architecture

Always.